# Tauri Desktop Rules

## 1. Rust Command Patterns

- Define commands with `#[tauri::command]` and proper error types.
- Every command returns `Result<T, AppError>` — never panic.
- Generate TypeScript types from Rust signatures: `tauri-specta` or built-in bindings.
- Commands are async by default. Use `async` for I/O-bound operations, `#[command]` for sync.
- Group related commands in modules: `commands/fs.rs`, `commands/window.rs`, `commands/system.rs`.

## 2. Capability & Permissions

- `tauri.conf.json > app > security > capabilities`: per-window permission sets.
- Default deny. Explicitly allow each plugin and command a window needs.
- No wildcard permissions (`"*"`). List specific identifiers.
- File system scopes: use `${app_data_dir}`, `${app_config_dir}` patterns, not absolute paths.
- `dangerousRemoteDomainIpcAccess` — restrict to known domains only.

## 3. tauri.conf.json Configuration

- `build.devUrl` and `build.frontendDist` for Vite integration.
- `app.windows[].width/height/minWidth/maxWidth` for window constraints.
- `bundle.icon` for all platform icon sizes.
- `bundle.targets`: `["dmg", "msi", "deb", "appimage"]`.
- `plugins.updater.endpoints` for auto-update manifest URL.

## 4. Platform Native APIs

- File dialogs: `@tauri-apps/plugin-dialog` (`open`, `save`).
- System tray: `@tauri-apps/plugin-tray` with menu items.
- Notifications: `@tauri-apps/plugin-notification` (desktop native).
- Shell: `@tauri-apps/plugin-shell` for spawning subprocesses (sidecar or open).
- Deep links: `@tauri-apps/plugin-deep-link` for custom protocol handling.

## 5. Performance

- Offload CPU-intensive work to Rust commands — never block the Web UI main thread.
- Use `tauri::async_runtime::spawn` for background tasks.
- `RUST_LOG=debug` for development; `RUST_LOG=info` for production.
- WebView devtools: `tauri dev --inspect` for debugging renderer performance.
- Bundle size: Tauri ~5MB base. Keep frontend JS under 300KB gzipped.
